transcript.content:
This is a very complete, well-shaped bundle. As a spec for “how to make two models explore without drifting into empty cleverness,” it’s already at a level where:

- a system designer can translate it to state + predicates, and  
- a dialogue model can treat it as a *normative stance* (how to behave), not just a checklist.

No extra structure is strictly needed to make it implementable. The only additions I’d consider are very small and mechanical:

1. **Minimal state schema (for an engineer):**

   ```text
   DialogueState {
     turns: [TurnState];  // rolling window, e.g., last 8
     target_regime: {E, A, B};
   }

   TurnState {
     text: string;
     phase_effect: {expand, constrain, compress, shift, none};
     progress_delta: {RC, CDJ, compression, none};
     metrics_adopted: [MetricHandle];
     harsh_rule_flag: bool;
     care_benefit_pass: bool | unknown;
   }

   MetricHandle {
     name: string;
     has_ITL: bool;
     has_calibration: bool;
   }
   ```

   The micro-checklist becomes pure logic over this.

2. **A single meta-prompt pattern you can reuse:**

   Something like:

   > “Review the last N turns.  
   > - Did we add a new lens that changes evidence/options/success?  
   > - Did we add any real boundaries or tests?  
   > - Did we commit, even reversibly, to dropping/parking anything?  
   > If not, do *one* of those now.  
   > If metrics or rules appeared, explain which target (E/A/B) they serve, how they could mislead, and when we’d revise them.”

   This one meta-prompt, called every K turns, is enough to keep the 3C+S + deltas + ITL machinery “on” without hard-coding all details.

If you wired that into a system—state tracking + your spec + a periodic meta-prompt—you’d have a genuinely distinctive interaction style:

- creative and wandering when it can be,  
- but periodically forced to say what changed, what was dropped, and how any scoring rule is answerable to a declared notion of insight.

From here, the next natural layer isn’t more theory; it’s empirical: run a few real model–model dialogues under this protocol in each domain and see what failure modes remain. But as a design document, this is already unusually complete and directly usable.
